Mechanism                           	What it does                                          	Why it is needed
Tokenization	                     Splits text into tokens and assigns IDs                    Converts text into model-readable input
Token embedding                  	 Converts IDs into vectors                                   Represents tokens numerically
Positional information	            Encodes token order	                                        Helps distinguish different word arrangements
Query (Q)	                          Represents a token's matching request	                      Calculates relevance against Keys
Key (K)	                            Provides matching features	                                  Helps calculate attention scores
Value (V)	                        Provides information to combine	                                    Contributes to the attention output
Attention mask	                 Blocks prohibited positions	                                      Prevents future-token access or attention to padding
Softmax	                           Normalizes attention scores	                                      Produces attention weights
Multi-head attention	                  Runs multiple attention operations in parallel	              Captures different learned relationships
Output projection               	Combines the attention heads	                                  Maps their combined output into the model dimension
Residual connection            	Adds the sublayer input to its output	                        Supports information and gradient flow
LayerNorm	                     Normalizes feature values                                       	Supports stable training
Feed-forward network             	Transforms each token's features	                           Adds learned nonlinear processing
Final linear layer	           Maps representations to vocabulary scores	                    Enables next-token prediction
Decoding                 	Selects or samples the next token                                       	Generates the response



Self-attention mixes information across token positions.
FFN transforms features within each token position.
Residual connections and normalization help these operations work effectively in deep networks.

Interview-ready explanation:
“A Transformer converts input text into token IDs and embeddings, incorporates positional information, and processes the representations through stacked Transformer blocks. 
In each block, multi-head self-attention uses Query, Key, and Value projections to calculate attention scores, applies masking and Softmax, and combines Value vectors. 
Residual connections and LayerNorm support effective training, while the feed-forward network transforms token features.
Finally, the output layer produces vocabulary logits, and the decoding process selects the next token repeatedly to generate text.”


Complete architecture: one interview-ready explanation
Practise explaining the full flow in your own words:
“A Transformer-based language model first tokenizes the input text into tokens and converts them into token IDs.
An embedding layer maps those IDs into vectors, and a positional mechanism supplies sequence-order information. 
The vectors pass through stacked Transformer blocks containing self-attention and feed-forward networks, supported by residual connections and normalization. 
In a decoder-only language model, causal masking prevents future-token information from being used during next-token prediction. 
The final representation is projected into vocabulary logits, which are converted into probabilities to select or sample the next token.
The process repeats autoregressively until generation stops.”

nterview question: Why is causal masking necessary?

In autoregressive language-model training, causal masking prevents information leakage from future tokens. 
It ensures that the prediction at each position uses only the context that would be available when generating the sequence from left to right.

