A. Encoder-only — BERT
Understand text
The encoder uses self-attention that can access tokens on both sides of a token. This is called bidirectional attention.

Example: Classify a customer message as a complaint, query, or request. Extract a person's name or identify sentiment.

Why useful? It builds representations of the input text rather than primarily generating a response token by token.


B. Decoder-only — GPT-style LLMs
Generate text
The decoder uses causal self-attention. Each token can attend to earlier tokens, but not future tokens.

Example: Answer a banking question, generate Python code, or write a customer email.

Why useful? It learns to predict the next token from the tokens that came before it.


C. Encoder-decoder — original Transformer
Transform input into output
The encoder processes the input. The decoder generates the output using masked self-attention and cross-attention to the encoder's output.

Example: Translate “Good morning” into another language.

Why useful? It separates understanding the source sequence from generating the target sequence.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A. Encoder-only — understand text
Understanding
Choose this when the main output is a classification, embedding, or extracted information rather than a long generated response.

Banking project examples:
- Classify a complaint as a payment, KYC, or fraud issue.
- Create embeddings for semantic search.
- Identify account numbers or organization names in documents.
Models: BERT, RoBERTa, embedding models.

Typical use: classification, semantic search, named entity recognition. 

B. Decoder-only — generate text
Generation
Choose this when the model needs to generate responses one token at a time.

Banking project examples:
- Build a banking customer-support chatbot.
- Generate answers from retrieved policy documents (RAG).
- Summarize a customer case or draft a support response.
Models: GPT, Llama, Mistral.

Typical use: chatbots, text generation, code generation and agentic AI. 


C. Encoder-decoder — transform input into output
Sequence-to-sequence
Choose this when a model reads an input sequence and generates a corresponding output sequence.

Banking project examples:
- Translate banking policies into another language.
- Rewrite complex policy text into simpler language.
- Convert a long document into a concise summary.
Models: T5, BART, the original Transformer.

Typical use: translation, summarization and text-to-text transformation
